Chapter 7

Plaintiff’s Conduct and
Apportioning Responsibility

■   ■   ■

Up to now, we have generally concentrated on a conventional lawsuit between an injured plaintiff and a defendant charged with responsibility for causing plaintiff’s injury. As we have often seen, however, injury cases are much more complicated, with many potential defendants who have in some way contributed to plaintiff’s injury. Moreover, plaintiff’s conduct may have also contributed to the injury. In this chapter we take up some of the many problems raised by these more complicated situations.

Plaintiff determines who to sue and on what theories of liability. Defendants raise challenges to the plaintiff’s prima facie case, but also may have affirmative defenses that raise either the plaintiff’s own conduct as a contributing cause of the harm, or the conduct of others. You will recall from Chapter 5, Causation, that traditionally multiple defendants were treated as jointly and severally liable for harm to a plaintiff based on theories that permitted liability to be established against a group of defendants. Sometimes, doctrines of proximate cause acted to limit liability in these cases. When a plaintiff was contributorily negligent, as in cases such as Martin v. Herzog, supra at page 227, that negligence traditionally was a total bar to recovery. These doctrines—of joint and several liability and contributory negligence—have the potential to impose or limit liability unfairly. Over the history of the common law, the courts, sometimes aided by legislative intervention, have addressed many of the complex issues of fairness and proper allocation of responsibility, leading many to adopt principles of apportionment of responsibility and comparative fault.

We start with plaintiff’s contributory conduct, a classic affirmative defense that a defendant will raise and, thus, has the burden of proving. Then we turn to the evolution of the law regarding liability of multiple defendants. We finish the chapter by looking at some special problems raised by the adoption of apportionment principles in most U.S. jurisdictions.

A. CONTRIBUTORY NEGLIGENCE
Until the middle of the twentieth century, almost all states recognized the doctrine of contributory negligence. If the plaintiff was found to have been at fault to any degree in causing the accident, the plaintiff’s behavior
720
was a complete defense to a negligence action. Recall that this was the rule adopted by Justice Shaw in Brown v. Kendall, page 163. In the intervening years, however, at least forty-six states and the United States, in many tort related federal statutory actions, adopted some general form of comparative negligence, in which the plaintiff, although partially at fault in causing the accident may be able to recover some portion of her damages. Because the common law doctrine still exists substantially unaltered in some states and because many of the subsequent developments require understanding of the common law doctrine, we begin with a review of contributory negligence as it existed, until recently, in most jurisdictions in this country. Understand also that the law concerning what constitutes contributory negligence has not, for the most part, been altered with the adoption of comparative negligence. Only the portion of responsibility and, thus, the amount of damages that the plaintiff can recover has changed.

Butterfield v. Forrester

King’s Bench, 1809.
11 East 59, 103 Eng.Rep. 926.

This was an action on the case for obstructing a highway, by means of which obstruction the plaintiff, who was riding along the road, was thrown down with his horse, and injured, & c. At the trial before Bayley J. at Derby, it appeared that the defendant, for the purpose of making some repairs to his house, which was close by the road side at one end of the town, had put up a pole across this part of the road, a free passage being left by another branch or street in the same direction. That the plaintiff left a public house not far distant from the place in question at 8 o’clock in the evening in August, when they were just beginning to light candles, but while there was light enough left to discern the obstruction at 100 yards distance: and the witness, who proved this, said that if the plaintiff had not been riding very hard he might have observed and avoided it: the plaintiff however, who was riding violently, did not observe it, but rode against it, and fell with his horse and was much hurt in consequence of the accident; and there was no evidence of his being intoxicated at the time. On this evidence Bayley J. directed the jury, that if a person riding with reasonable and ordinary care could have seen and avoided the obstruction; and if they were satisfied that the plaintiff was riding along the street extremely hard, and without ordinary care, they should find a verdict for the defendant: which they accordingly did.

Vaughan Serjt. now objected to this direction, on moving for a new trial; and referred to Buller’s Ni. Pri. 26(a), where the rule is laid down, that “if a man lay logs of wood across a highway; though a person may with care ride safely by, yet if by means thereof my horse stumble and fling me, I may bring an action.”

721
Bayley J. The plaintiff was proved to be riding as fast as his horse could go, and this was through the streets of Derby. If he had used ordinary care he must have seen the obstruction; so that the accident appeared to happen entirely from his own fault.

Lord Ellenborough C.J. A party is not to cast himself upon an obstruction which has been made by the fault of another, and avail himself of it, if he do not himself use common and ordinary caution to be in the right. In cases of persons riding upon what is considered to be the wrong side of the road, that would not authorise another purposely to ride up against them. One person being in fault will not dispense with another’s using ordinary care for himself. Two things must concur to support this action, an obstruction in the road by the fault of the defendant, and no want of ordinary care to avoid it on the part of the plaintiff.

Per Curiam. Rule refused.

Notes

1.Among the early cases in which the plaintiff’s contributory negligence barred his recovery against a negligent defendant are: Flower v. Adam, 2 Taunt. 314, 127 Eng.Rep. 1098 (C.P.1810); Smith v. Smith, 19 Mass. (2 Pick.) 621 (1824); Pennsylvania Railroad Co. v. Aspell, 23 Pa. 147 (1854). In the Aspell case, the trial court had instructed the jury that the plaintiff’s “imprudence” might be considered in assessing damages—a suggestion found in several early cases—but the Supreme Court of Pennsylvania held that the plaintiff’s action was completely barred and this became the prevailing rule. This is the aspect of the doctrine that has been changed substantially by the advent of comparative fault. It will be taken up in detail later in this chapter.

2.What is the rationale behind the contributory negligence as a total bar to recovery rule? Doesn’t it permit a negligent defendant to be relieved from liability even though negligence of others is foreseeable and does not limit duty? What goal does the “total bar to recovery” rule serve?

3.In the nineteenth century many states made proof of the absence of contributory negligence part of the plaintiff’s case. Brown v. Kendall, in Chapter 3, reflects the early confusion. Most states now place the burden of persuasion on the issue of contributory negligence upon the defendant (and this remains true with the advent of comparative negligence). In other words, contributory negligence/comparative negligence is treated as an affirmative defense. See Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability § 4.

4.As we have seen in Martin v. Herzog, supra page 227, violation of a statute can constitute contributory negligence as a matter of law. See also Southern Pacific Co. v. Castro, 493 S.W.2d 491 (Tex.1973).

5.Contributory negligence will normally also be a defense to an action based upon the defendant’s violation of a statutory duty. It will not, however, be a defense to an action based upon a violation of a statute if (i) the statute imposes absolute liability, i.e. requires no scienter on the part of the defendant,
722
see Jenkins v. Chicago & Eastern Illinois Railroad, 284 N.E.2d 392 (Ill. 1972); (ii) the statute is aimed at protecting a limited class of persons against their own imprudence, such as children or intoxicated persons, see Zerby v. Warren, 210 N.W.2d 58 (Minn. 1973) (superseded by Child Labor Standards Act); Chainani v. Board of Education of the City of New York, 663 N.E.2d 283 (N.Y. 1995); or (iii) the plaintiff is a worker injured as a result of the violation of a factory safety act, see Koenig v. Patrick Construction Corp., 83 N.E.2d 133 (N.Y. 1948). These three categories often overlap.

6.As already indicated in Chapter 3, in the Goss case and notes following it, the issue of contributory negligence is supposed to be governed by the same objective standard that is used to determine whether a defendant has been negligent. Compare Restatement (Second) of Torts § 464 with id. § 483. This principle is continued in the Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability § 3.

Some jurisdictions treated the standard of care differently, however, for plaintiffs with a mental disability, see Mochen v. State, 43 A.D.2d 484, 352 N.Y.S.2d 290 (1974) (a person with severe mental disability or retardation is only required to exercise the care for his own person of which he is capable). With the advent of comparative fault, however, there would seem to be little reason to judge the conduct of the plaintiff and the defendant by different standards. The jury would be likely to take such factors into account in allocating responsibility. Moreover, the use of different standards would make it more difficult to compare the fault of the plaintiff with that of the defendant. The Restatement (Third) reflects these ideas. It explicitly rejects the modification of the standard as to plaintiff’s conduct. See Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability § 3, comment (a), providing that the same standard used for assessing defendant’s conduct will also be used to assess the plaintiff’s. This rule may not be applicable in cases where a caretaker of a mentally ill patient is the defendant. Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 7. What is the reason for this exception? See Colman v. Notre Dame Convalescent Home, Inc., 968 F.Supp. 809 (D. Conn. 1997) and Cowan v. Doering, 545 A.2d 159 (N.J. 1988). See generally, Sarah Light, Rejecting the Logic of Confinement: Care Relationships and the Mentally Disabled Under Tort Law, 109 Yale L.J. 381 (1999).

7.Courts commonly carved out several exceptions to the contributory negligence rule. It was generally held that plaintiff’s contributory negligence was not a defense if the defendant’s conduct was intentional, willful or wanton, or even grossly negligent. However, if a jury found that the plaintiff’s misconduct measured up to the same degree of fault as the defendant the contributory negligence rule would once again apply. For example, consider the unhappy fate of Frank Elliott when he attempted to charge wanton and willful misconduct by the defendant to save himself from his own negligent behavior. As reported by the Pennsylvania Supreme Court, the facts were as follows:

At about 10:30 a.m., May 27, 1943, a clear day, plaintiff, a paper hanger by trade, was at the northwest corner of Haverford Avenue and Preston Street when he heard and saw a fire engine about a block
723
away on Preston Street approaching southward toward Haverford Avenue; it was returning to the fire house on the northeast corner of Preston Street and Haverford Avenue. He also saw a street car approaching from the west on Haverford Avenue. He testified that on seeing the fire engine, he ‘went out in the middle of the street [Haverford Avenue] and I stood there right in the middle of the car track’ and ‘waved to the trolley car’ which was then ‘at 41st and Haverford Avenue.’ The distance between him and 41st Street was 421 feet. He was familiar with the neighborhood. He gave the speed of the trolley car as ‘about 25 or 30 miles an hour.’ At first he was ‘facing the trolley car’ but after a while he turned, facing northward, and ‘waved for the fire engine to come on, and the trolley car kept moving toward me.’ The car ‘was coming about the same speed, around 30 miles an hour.’ When the car struck him he was ‘looking toward the fire engine.’ The car stopped within its own length. The plaintiff had an unobstructed view as there was no other traffic on Haverford Avenue between 41st Street and the plaintiff. He testified that he had been facing the approaching car until it was between Budd Street and Ludwick Street, when he ‘turned around and had my hands up and waved for the fire engine to come on * * *.’ Budd Street was 232 feet from Preston Street where plaintiff stood and Ludwick Street was 75 feet from there. At the time he turned from facing the street car it must then have been at some point within the 232 feet distance between Budd and Preston Streets and must reach him within five or six seconds at the rate at which he said it was moving.

There was no reason why he remained standing in the car track to give the signals he described; he was not asked to give them. He appears to have volunteered to ‘flag’ the fire engine into Haverford Avenue. There is nothing to show that such ‘flagging’ was needed nor that the motorman on the street car should have known what plaintiff intended by ‘waving’ his arms. He might have given his signals just as well and in perfect safety while standing in the Avenue outside the range of the rapidly approaching car. He continued to stand in the tracks although a step or two would have taken him out of danger. By remaining on the track he also endangered the street car passengers.

* * *

Plaintiff’s reckless conduct bars recovery for the result of defendant’s wanton misconduct for the same reason that contributory negligence constitutes a defense to an action based on mere negligence. Contributory negligence is a defense because it introduces in a case a new cause of plaintiff’s injury making defendant’s negligence no longer the legal cause. So in this case, defendant’s wanton misconduct, as a legal cause of injury, was
724
superseded by plaintiff’s wanton or reckless misconduct which became the legal cause of his injury.

Elliott v. Philadelphia Transp. Co., 53 A.2d 81, 82–83 (Pa. 1947). How would you characterize the plaintiff’s behavior? How would you characterize the behavior of the trolley operator? Does the following testimony, found in the dissenting opinion, change your assessment: “After the accident, the driver of the fire truck asked the [trolley] motorman: ‘What is the idea of hitting that man? Didn’t you see that man?’ The motorman replied: ‘Sure, I saw the man, but what the hell is he doing on the track?’ ” Id. at 84.

8.Causation. It is often said, as in Elliott, that plaintiff’s contributory negligence bars recovery because it takes over from the defendant’s negligence as the cause of harm. Plaintiff’s contributory negligence must, therefore, be an actual cause of harm to support this conclusion. Remember, again, Martin v. Herzog supra at page 227. If there had been sufficient light at the time of the accident for the defendant in Martin to be able to see the plaintiff’s buggy regardless of the failure to have lights, the plaintiff’s failure would not have contributed to his injury, and he would not have been barred from recovery. Defendant must establish the causal link between plaintiff’s negligence and his harm.

Consider also Smithwick v. Hall & Upson Co., 21 A. 924 (Conn. 1890). Plaintiff had been instructed not to work on an unsafe end of scaffolding. Plaintiff unreasonably worked on the unsafe end of the scaffolding. The brick walls of the building on which plaintiff was working fell on him, causing him to fall and suffer serious injuries. Defendant raised contributory negligence. The plaintiff argued, and the court agreed, that the plaintiff’s negligence did not cause his harm because he would have suffered serious harm by the falling of the bricks regardless of where he was standing on the scaffolding. In addition, the risk of harm from the plaintiff’s conduct was falling as a result of the unsafe scaffolding, not the risk of having bricks fall on him while working, of which he was entirely unaware and which did not render his conduct negligent. What is the issue here? Cause-in-fact or proximate cause?

A more recent case in which the proximate cause issue was raised is DeMoss v. Hamilton, 644 N.W.2d 302 (Iowa 2002). The plaintiff, as the result of a previous heart attack had been advised to quit using tobacco, engage in exercise, and eat properly to reduce cholesterol. His failure to do so, however, was not a relevant cause of his death for purposes of a suit for malpractice against a doctor that failed to diagnose and treat a subsequent heart attack. A charge inviting the jury to consider plaintiff’s negligence was error.

9.Last clear chance. Another important exception to the contributory negligence as a total bar rule is the “last clear chance” doctrine. As its name implies, if the negligent defendant had the “last clear chance” to avoid harm to the contributorily negligent plaintiff, then the plaintiff may recover in spite of his negligence. The idea is that the plaintiff’s negligence is no longer the cause of harm because the defendant ultimately could have avoided the harm and so should remain liable for it.

725
The doctrine of last clear chance developed very early in the history of contributory negligence. It recognized that negligently caused injuries often result from a sequence of events. The courts tried to allocate legal blame for the injury to the last party that had a chance to avoid the accident. It worked primarily as a device to soften the impact of plaintiff’s contributory negligence when the defendant had perceived the perilous position of the plaintiff (or in some manifestations of the doctrine, could have perceived) and could have avoided the accident by the exercise of reasonable care. This sequential analysis was very effective at putting the burden on the party who was the best accident avoider in many situations. The first case to articulate this idea was Davies v. Mann, 10 M. & W. 546, 152 Eng.Rep. 588 (Exch. 1842). Davies involved the unfortunate death of the plaintiff’s negligently fettered donkey, run over as it was grazing on the side of the road by the careless defendant’s wagon. In his well-known treatise, Dean Prosser referred to last clear chance as “the jackass doctrine,” a pejorative having obvious dual meanings in view of the great difficulties it caused both judges and lawyers in the ensuing years.

The advent of comparative negligence makes the last clear chance doctrine largely irrelevant. Only jurisdictions that retain contributory negligence may continue to apply last clear chance in its traditional form. The Restatement (Second) of Torts sections 479 and 480 provide the traditional rules, explaining the doctrine in terms of the helpless and inattentive plaintiff. When the plaintiff is helpless and the defendant actually discovers the plaintiff’s peril, almost all American jurisdictions permitted the plaintiff to recover. In such situations some courts were prepared to characterize the defendant’s conduct as “willful and wanton.” Where the plaintiff was helpless and the defendant did not actually discover the plaintiff’s peril but reasonably should have, a majority of jurisdictions applied the doctrine. Likewise, where the plaintiff is inattentive and the defendant is aware of the plaintiff’s situation, a majority of courts followed the Restatement position. The predominant view is that last clear chance, as a separate doctrine, does not survive the introduction of comparative fault.

